---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import WordToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/WordToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="word-to-pdf">
  <WordToPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a Word document to PDF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop a <code>.docx</code> file onto the box above. Mammoth unzips and reads it in this tab.</li>
      <li>Check the structure counts: words, headings, paragraphs and list items found in the document.</li>
      <li>Choose the page size, typeface, body text size and margin. Headings scale automatically from the body size.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Create PDF</strong>, then download. Change a setting and press Rebuild to try another layout.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What the Word to PDF conversion keeps</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Element</th>
            <th>Result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Paragraphs</td><td>Preserved, wrapped and paginated</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Headings 1–6</td><td>Preserved, set in bold at a proportional size</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bold and italic runs</td><td>Preserved, including bold italic</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Inline code and monospaced runs</td><td>Preserved as Courier</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bullet and numbered lists</td><td>Preserved, including nesting and hanging indents</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Blockquotes</td><td>Preserved, indented with a vertical rule</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Horizontal rules</td><td>Preserved</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tables</td><td>Redrawn with proportional columns and a bold header row; merged cells are lost</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Images, charts, shapes</td><td>Skipped; the count is shown before you export</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Headers, footers, footnotes, page numbers from Word</td><td>Not carried over; this tool can add its own page numbers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Columns, text boxes, fields, comments, tracked changes</td><td>Not carried over</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fonts, colours, highlighting</td><td>Replaced by your chosen standard typeface in black</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why the PDF is typeset again</h2>
    <p>
      When Word exports a PDF, it uses its own layout engine and the fonts installed on your computer, and
      copies the document glyph for glyph. A browser has neither. This tool reads the document's structure and
      sets it again: Mammoth parses the OOXML into semantic HTML, the tool turns that into typesetting
      instructions, and pdf-lib lays them out with real font metrics for word wrapping and pagination.
    </p>
    <p>
      The result is a tidy, readable PDF with selectable text and the same content as the original. It is not
      pixel-identical. When exact layout matters, as for a signed contract or a designed brochure, use Word's
      own <strong>Save as PDF</strong>. For a clean PDF of a document's content without an upload, use this
      tool.
    </p>

    <h2>Fonts and non-Latin text</h2>
    <p>
      The three typefaces here, Helvetica, Times and Courier, are among the PDF standard's base 14 fonts, so they
      are not embedded in the file. Every reader already has them. This keeps the output small (often under
      100 KB for a long document) and makes it look the same everywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cost is character coverage: those fonts use WinAnsi encoding, which covers Western European
      alphabets. Greek, Cyrillic, Hebrew, Arabic, Devanagari, CJK and emoji cannot be drawn. The converter
      transliterates what it can, by removing diacritics and mapping typographic dashes and quotes, and reports
      how many characters it replaced with a question mark, so you know whether the output is usable.
    </p>

    <h2>Only .docx, not .doc</h2>
    <p>
      The modern <code>.docx</code> format is a ZIP of XML parts that a browser can read. The legacy
      <code>.doc</code> format is a different binary structure from Word 97, and no browser library reads it
      reliably. If you have a <code>.doc</code>, open it in a word processor and save it as <code>.docx</code>
      first. The tool detects the old format and tells you.
    </p>
    <p>
      To convert the other way, use <a href="/pdf-to-word">PDF to Word</a>. For plain text or Markdown, use
      <a href="/text-to-pdf">Text to PDF</a> and <a href="/markdown-to-pdf">Markdown to PDF</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
